import pf.Html
import ../../Ui/Heading
import ../../Ui/Callout
import ../../Ui/Code
import ../../Generated/Sources
import ../../Generated/Paths
import ../../Ui/PageHero
import ../../Ui/TableWrap

meta = {
	title: "Tooling",
	description: "The rocx command: dev, build (and a static copy), check (an app, a command's reports, its SQL), lint, routes, fmt and qr; compiler errors reported at your .rocx lines; editor support; stories; building an app by hand for production.",
	toc: [
		{ id: "commands", label: "Commands" },
		{ id: "errors", label: "Errors at your lines" },
		{ id: "editors", label: "In an editor" },
		{ id: "stories", label: "Stories" },
		{ id: "setup", label: "Building an app yourself" },
		{ id: "repository", label: "This repository" },
	],
}

view! = |_|
	<>
		<NoHydration>
			<PageHero
				eyebrow="Tooling"
				title="A few commands, and the compiler you already have"
			>
				Everything Roc Solid does for you as you work goes through one command, <code>rocx</code>
				(<code>bin/rocx</code> in the repository, itself written in Roc). It turns markup into Roc,
				builds and serves your app while you edit it, and points the Roc compiler's reports at your
				<code>.rocx</code> lines. The Roc compiler does the rest. This page covers each command, how
				errors are reported, editor support, stories, and how to build an app for production by hand.
			</PageHero>

			<Heading id="commands">Commands</Heading>
			<p>
				A <code>dir</code> is an app's folder, the one with <code>routes/</code> in it; without one, a
				command works on the current folder. Run <code>rocx</code> alone to see the usage line.
			</p>
			<div class="commands">
				<div class="command">
					<p class="command-usage">rocx dev {"[dir]"}<span>--port 8080</span><span>--android {"[--lan]"}</span><span>-- {"<server options>"}</span></p>
					<div>
						<p>
							Builds the app and serves it at <code>http://localhost:8080</code> in development mode.
							Each time you save a file, it builds the app again, restarts the server and reloads its
							open pages. While a build fails, its errors are shown at their <code>.rocx</code> lines
							and the last good build keeps running. The first run writes a <code>main.roc</code> if the
							app has none: the smallest program, yours to change afterwards. After each start it checks
							the app's SQL, as <code>rocx check --sql</code> does.
						</p>
						<p>
							The port is <code>--port</code>, else <code>PORT</code>, else 8080. What follows
							<code>--</code> goes to the server (<code>--database-url postgres://…</code>), which also
							reads its environment (<code>DATABASE_URL</code>). Development mode serves the
							<a href="#stories">stories</a> at <code>/__rocx/stories</code>, plugins' development pages
							(the email package's <a href={"${Paths.ecosystem_email}#mailbox"}>mailbox</a>), and a
							crash's message in its 500 answer. Builds use Roc's development backend, which compiles
							fast; <a href="#setup">building it yourself</a> makes an optimized one.
						</p>
						<p>
							<code>--android</code> builds each change for an Android phone too: it installs a
							development client on an emulator or a phone on USB, and reloads it at each save.
							<code>--lan</code> serves the app on your network as well, and prints a QR code that a
							phone on the same Wi-Fi scans. It needs the Android SDK and NDK; the
							<a href={Paths.examples_pomodoro}>pomodoro</a> example runs this way.
						</p>
					</div>
				</div>
				<div class="command">
					<p class="command-usage">rocx build {"<dir>…"}<span>--client {"<out>"}</span><span>--dev</span></p>
					<div>
						<p>
							Turns each <code>.rocx</code> file under the folders into the <code>.roc</code> file beside
							it, its markup written as calls to the platform's <code>Html</code>. For an app it also
							writes <code>Generated/</code>: <code>Routes.roc</code> (the router), <code>Paths.roc</code>
							(a typed link for each route), <code>Api.roc</code> and <code>Handlers.roc</code> (the
							endpoints' contract, and their handlers), <code>Styles.roc</code> (the stylesheet),
							<code>App.roc</code> (the program that <code>main.roc</code> completes) and, for an app with
							pages, <code>Root.roc</code> (the page at the browser's path, in its layouts). A route
							file's own Roc goes there too. A package's components are copied into
							<code>{"Generated/<package>/"}</code>, and each folder under <code>content/</code> becomes
							<code>{"Generated/Content/<Name>.roc"}</code>, its Markdown files listed.
						</p>
						<p>
							<code>--client</code> also writes the browser's copy of the app into
							<code>{"<out>"}</code>. In it, an endpoint is only its method and URL, a route file has no
							middleware, a <code>{"<NoHydration>"}</code> has no children, and the imports only those used
							are gone. For an app with pages, the copy has the browser's <code>main.roc</code> and
							<code>static/rocx/</code>: <code>start.js</code>, the runtime, and the app's stylesheet,
							named by its content (<code>styles-<var>hash</var>.css</code>). The runtime is minified
							(its comments and indentation gone: about half its size with brotli), but in a
							<code>--dev</code> build, which keeps it as it is written, to read and debug. An app of endpoints alone
							has no browser program, and the build says so. If the browser's copy reaches a module that
							imports what only the server has (<code>pf.Db</code>, directly or through another module),
							the build warns at the import a page or component makes, before the browser's build would
							fail far from it.
						</p>
						<p><code>--dev</code> also builds the <a href="#stories">stories</a> and their catalog.</p>
					</div>
				</div>
				<div class="command" id="static">
					<p class="command-usage">rocx build {"<dir>"}<span>--static {"<out>"}</span><span>--base /path/</span><span>--route {"<path>"}</span><span>--built {"<build>"}</span><span>-- {"<server options>"}</span></p>
					<div>
						<p>
							Writes the app as static files into <code>{"<out>"}</code>, for a host with no server:
							GitHub Pages, a CDN, any folder a web server serves. It builds the app for production,
							starts its server on a free port, and asks it for every page: <code>/</code>, each page
							whose path has no params, and every page those link to. Each page becomes
							<code>{"<path>/index.html"}</code>. Beside them go <code>404.html</code> (what a path
							nothing answers shows), the app's <code>static/</code>, and <code>rocx/</code> with the
							runtime and the wasm. A page carries the answers to the reads it made while it rendered,
							so its data is there without a server. This site is built this way.
						</p>
						<p>
							<code>--base</code> puts every URL under a path, for a site served there
							(<code>example.github.io/docs/</code> is <code>--base /docs/</code>). <code>--route</code>
							asks for a path no page links to: a page with params (<code>/blog/hello</code>), or an
							endpoint's answer, written as a file (<code>/feed.xml</code>). <code>--built</code> uses a
							build you made (its <code>server</code> and <code>client/static</code>) instead of building
							one. The copy is made beside <code>{"<out>"}</code> and replaces it only once it is
							complete; <code>{"<out>"}</code> must be empty, or an earlier copy.
						</p>
						<p>
							What only a server can answer isn't in the copy, and the build lists it: a method that
							writes (<code>post!</code>), events and channels, a page whose route has middleware of
							its own (it answers each request as it comes), a page with params that no link or
							<code>--route</code> reached, and a link to a path that answered an error.
						</p>
					</div>
				</div>
				<div class="command">
					<p class="command-usage">rocx check {"[dir]"}</p>
					<p>
						Checks the app from its sources: it builds the app as <code>rocx dev</code> does, then
						type-checks the server's program and, for an app with pages, the browser's. Every problem is
						shown at its <code>.rocx</code> line, and nothing an older build left behind is read. It
						exits 0 when all is well, 1 on an error, and 2 on warnings alone, rocx's own among them: a
						<code>match</code> arm's value or a list row's item given to a component that reads it only
						once (<a href={"${Paths.components}#control"}>if, match and .map</a>). The app needs a
						<code>main.roc</code>. Run it in CI.
					</p>
				</div>
				<div class="command">
					<p class="command-usage">rocx check -- {"<command>"}</p>
					<p>
						Runs a command (<code>roc check</code>, <code>roc build</code>, <code>just check</code>) and
						points the compiler reports it prints into the <code>.rocx</code> files
						(<a href="#errors">errors at your lines</a>). It exits with the command's code.
					</p>
				</div>
				<div class="command">
					<p class="command-usage">rocx check --sql {"[dir]"}<span>--engine sqlite|turso</span><span>--verbose</span><span>-- {"<server options>"}</span></p>
					<div>
						<p>
							Checks the app's SQL with its own database engine, and the rows it reads with the compiler.
							Each statement written as a string at a <code>Db.all!</code> or <code>Db.run!</code> is
							prepared on a scratch database built from the app's migrations: for SQLite or Turso, a file
							it deletes afterwards; for Postgres, a database it creates on the server
							<code>--database-url</code> (or <code>DATABASE_URL</code>) names, and drops afterwards.
							Reported at their lines: what the engine refuses, a call whose parameters don't match its
							statement, and a row type that doesn't fit the columns. It exits 1 if anything is wrong
							(<a href={"${Paths.platform_database}#checked"}>how it works</a>).
						</p>
						<p>
							<code>--engine</code> checks a SQLite app on Turso, or a Turso app on SQLite.
							<code>--verbose</code> also lists the calls it couldn't check, such as SQL built at run
							time. <code>rocx dev</code> runs this check after each build.
						</p>
					</div>
				</div>
				<div class="command">
					<p class="command-usage">rocx lint {"<dir>…"}</p>
					<p>
						Refuses <code>Html.source_text</code> in hand-written <code>.roc</code> files under the
						folders: it would put any string in a page as markup
						(<a href={"${Paths.safety}#lint"}>why</a>). A comment or a string that only mentions it
						doesn't count. It exits 1 if a file calls it.
					</p>
				</div>
				<div class="command">
					<p class="command-usage">rocx routes {"<dir>"}</p>
					<p>
						Lists an app's routes: each method and path, the file that answers it, and the middleware
						that file adds after the app's default
						(<a href={"${Paths.routing}#middleware"}>what runs, in what order</a>).
					</p>
				</div>
				<div class="command">
					<p class="command-usage">rocx fmt {"<path>…"}<span>--check</span></p>
					<p>
						Formats the Roc in each <code>.rocx</code> file, and each <code>.roc</code> file rocx didn't
						write, under the paths, with <code>roc fmt</code> (the one <code>ROC</code> names, else the
						<code>roc</code> on your <code>PATH</code>). Markup, and the Roc in its holes, stay as
						written: a piece of markup moves only with the code around it, and the text of a
						<code>{"<pre>"}</code> or <code>{"<textarea>"}</code> never moves. <code>--check</code> only
						lists the files that aren't formatted, and exits 1 if there are any.
					</p>
				</div>
				<div class="command">
					<p class="command-usage">rocx qr {"<text>"}<span>--pbm</span></p>
					<p>
						Prints the text as a QR code in the terminal, as <code>rocx dev --android --lan</code> does
						for a phone, or as a PBM image with <code>--pbm</code>. A code holds 106 bytes at most.
					</p>
				</div>
			</div>
			<p>
				<code>rocx build</code> writes a file only when its content changes, so a watcher that sees it
				doesn't rebuild forever. It deletes the <code>.roc</code> files it wrote whose
				<code>.rocx</code> file is gone, and stops at the first markup problem, with its line and column.
				The files it writes end with a <code># rocx: generated from …</code> line. Keep them out of
				version control: ignore <code>Generated/</code>, <code>build/</code> and the <code>.roc</code>
				files beside your <code>.rocx</code> files.
			</p>
			<p>Here is what <code>rocx routes</code> prints for the <a href={Paths.examples_hello}>hello</a> example:</p>
			<Code
				source={"$ rocx routes examples/hello\nMETHOD  PATH    FILE                      MIDDLEWARE (after the app's default)\nGET     /       routes/+page.rocx         -\nGET     /hello  routes/hello/+server.roc  -"}
				numbers={Bool.False}
				plain
			/>

			<Heading id="errors">Errors at your lines</Heading>
			<p>
				There are two kinds of mistake. A markup mistake (a tag that is never closed, an attribute rocx
				refuses) is rocx's own: <code>rocx build</code> reports it at its line and column, with a caret
				under it, and stops. Everything else is the Roc compiler's. Because line <var>N</var> of the
				generated file is line <var>N</var> of yours, the compiler's reports already name the right line.
				<code>rocx check</code> (and <code>rocx dev</code>) go further and make them read as if the
				compiler had seen your file: they rewrite the location, quote your line instead of the generated
				one, and move the caret onto the same code. A mistake in a route file's Roc
				(<code>+server.roc</code>) is reported at its line too.
			</p>
			<p>Here is a file with a type mistake in it:</p>
			<Code title="Posts.rocx" source={Sources.check_source} />
			<p>and what <code>rocx check -- roc check Posts.roc</code> printed for it when this page was built:</p>
			<div class="report"><Code title="The compiler, through rocx check" source={Sources.check_report} numbers={Bool.False} plain /></div>
			<Callout kind="tip" title="Reading a report">
				<p>
					The location is your <code>.rocx</code> file's line and column, the line quoted is yours, and
					the caret is under your own code. Fix it there: the generated <code>.roc</code> file is
					written again on the next build.
				</p>
			</Callout>

			<Heading id="editors">In an editor</Heading>
			<p>
				The repository's <code>editors/</code> folder has one grammar for <code>.rocx</code> files. It
				colours Roc by the official Roc grammar; markup (an element's tag, a component's as a type,
				attributes like <code>class:active</code> and <code>on:click.prevent</code>); the Roc in holes,
				and markup inside that Roc; and CSS in a <code>{"<style>"}</code>. The one guess it makes is
				whether a <code>{"<"}</code> starts markup: it does before a letter, unless a value is right
				before it. So <code>{"a <b"}</code>, a comparison with a space before <code>{"<"}</code> and none
				after, is coloured as markup.
			</p>
			<p>
				<strong>VS Code</strong>: <code>just vscode-extension</code> installs the extension, for every
				workspace (it needs <code>code</code> on your <code>PATH</code>, and Node). Besides the
				highlighting, it adds a task type. <em>Terminal → Run Task… → rocx</em> lists two tasks for each
				app in the workspace: <code>dev</code>, which runs <code>rocx dev</code> in a terminal of its
				own, and <code>check</code>, which runs <code>rocx check</code> once. What rocx and the compiler
				report shows at its <code>.rocx</code> line and in the Problems panel, and stays until the next
				run. A task of your own in <code>.vscode/tasks.json</code> runs any command the same way
				(<code>{"{ \"type\": \"rocx\", \"command\": \"just check\" }"}</code>). There is no language
				server yet: what the editor shows is what the build says.
			</p>
			<p>
				<strong>Orca</strong>: <code>just orca-plugin</code> installs a plugin that highlights
				<code>.rocx</code> and <code>.roc</code> files. It runs nothing, so it installs without asking
				for access. It shows no errors, since Orca has no way for a plugin to show them: use
				<code>rocx check</code>.
			</p>

			<Heading id="stories">Stories</Heading>
			<p>
				A component's stories are the ways it looks, written beside it, as Storybook's are. A
				<code>{"<story name=\"…\">"}</code> in the component's block, among its definitions, holds the
				markup of one. rocx makes each a definition in its place (<code>story_tip!</code> for
				<code>{"<story name=\"Tip\">"}</code>), so every build checks it against the component, but only a
				development build shows it: <code>rocx dev</code> (or <code>rocx build --dev</code>) serves a
				catalog at <code>/__rocx/stories</code>.
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.ui_callout.file} source={Sources.ui_callout.rocx} />
			<p>
				The catalog lists every story down the side, which folds away (<kbd>S</kbd>); <kbd>/</kbd>
				searches it, and <kbd>↑</kbd> and <kbd>↓</kbd> step through it. The story you pick shows in a
				frame, with the line it is written at, and can fill the window (<kbd>F</kbd>, and
				<kbd>Esc</kbd> to come back). A component's name shows all its stories side by side. Each story
				is the app's page at its own path (<code>/__rocx/stories/…</code>), rendered in the root layout
				and hydrated, so it works as it would in the app; a layout can tell it is a story by its path
				(this site shows a story with nothing around it). An app with no pages has nowhere to show one,
				and its stories are an error.
			</p>
			<p>
				An app's <code>stories/</code> folder holds modules of stories alone: drafts of something new, each
				a component with its own <code>{"<style>"}</code>, side by side in the catalog. Only a
				development build compiles that folder, so nothing in it is published, its styles included. This
				site's <code>site/stories/Landing.rocx</code> has the front page as it is, and drafts of it in six
				themes.
			</p>

			<Heading id="setup">Building an app yourself</Heading>
			<p>
				<code>rocx dev</code> builds both of an app's programs for you
				(<a href={"${Paths.root}#start"}>getting started</a>). For a build that ships, you run the steps
				yourself. An app is two programs (<a href={Paths.platform_}>the platform</a>): the server's, on the
				server platform (native code in a Rust host), and the browser's, the same pages on the browser
				platform (WebAssembly). <code>rocx build</code> writes the server's program,
				<code>Generated/App.roc</code>: every request goes through the app's middleware to a plugin's
				route or the router rocx writes. The app's own <code>main.roc</code> says only what rocx can't
				know:
			</p>
			<Code
				title="main.roc"
				source={"app [program] { pf: platform \"../rocx/server/platform/main.roc\" }\n\nimport pf.Middleware\nimport Generated/App\n\nprogram = App.program({\n\tinit!: |_started| Ok({}),\n\tmiddleware: Middleware.same_origin({}),\n})"}
			/>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<code>init!</code> makes the app's context once, when the server starts, and every request's
					middleware is given it.
				</li>
				<li>
					<code>middleware</code> runs around every request. The one <code>rocx dev</code> writes passes
					each request on; <code>Middleware.same_origin</code> refuses changes sent by other sites'
					pages (<a href={"${Paths.safety}#origin"}>why</a>).
				</li>
				<li>
					It may also say <code>database</code> (<code>Postgres</code> unless it says
					<code>Sqlite(path)</code> or <code>Turso(path)</code>), <code>openapi</code> (whether to serve
					the endpoints' OpenAPI document) and <code>plugins</code> (what packages add).
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				The browser's program is rocx's to write (<code>rocx build --client</code>): its root is the page
				at the browser's path, which the browser hydrates. This site's:
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.client_app.file} source={Sources.client_app.rocx} />
			<p>
				A release build is a few commands. Here they are as a Justfile, for an app in <code>app/</code>
				beside a clone of Roc Solid in <code>rocx/</code>:
			</p>
			<Code
				title="Justfile"
				source={"build:\n\t# The browser platform's host, a Zig object the wasm links with (once; rocx dev builds it too)\n\tzig build-obj rocx/web/host/host.zig -target wasm32-freestanding -O ReleaseSmall -fcompiler-rt -femit-bin=rocx/web/platform/targets/wasm32/host.wasm\n\trocx/bin/rocx build app --client build/client\n\trocx/.tools/roc-active build --target=wasm32 --opt=size --specialize=yes --output=build/client/static/rocx/app.wasm build/client/main.roc\n\tnode rocx/server/build.mjs app/main.roc build/server --opt=speed\n\nserve:\n\tbuild/server --port 8080 --static build/client/static\n\ncheck:\n\trocx/bin/rocx lint app\n\trocx/bin/rocx check app"}
				numbers={Bool.False}
				plain
			/>
			<ol>
				<li><code>rocx build --client</code> writes the Roc for both programs, and the browser's copy.</li>
				<li>
					<code>.tools/roc-active</code>, the Roc compiler <code>just setup</code> installed, compiles the
					browser's copy to wasm, beside <code>start.js</code>.
				</li>
				<li>
					<code>server/build.mjs</code> (Node) builds the Rust host, then the server's program, into one
					executable. <code>--target=arm64musl</code> or <code>x64musl</code> makes a static Linux
					executable for a container (<a href={"${Paths.platform_server}#deploying"}>deploying</a>).
				</li>
				<li>
					The executable serves the wasm and the rest of <code>build/client/static</code> with
					<code>--static</code> (add the app's own <code>static/</code> folder, if it has one). It
					takes its port, database and the rest as flags or environment variables
					(<a href={"${Paths.platform_server}#running"}>running it</a>).
				</li>
			</ol>
			<p>
				The two programs are optimized differently. The server's, <code>--opt=speed</code>, is built with
				boxy lowering (<code>--specialize=no</code>) by default. With lambda-set specialization, Roc's
				default, the function that renders an app's pages gets a stack frame that grows with the app,
				and a large app's could outgrow the stack a render runs on. Boxy keeps it small, renders about as
				fast and builds much faster. <code>--specialize=yes</code> (or <code>ROCX_SPECIALIZE=yes</code>)
				asks for specialization. The browser's wasm is better specialized: it is smaller with brotli and
				renders faster, which is why the Justfile above passes <code>--specialize=yes</code>.
			</p>
			<p>
				Requests, routes, endpoints, events and middleware are both platforms'
				(<code>pf.Server</code>, <code>pf.Endpoint</code>, <code>pf.Middleware</code>,
				<code>pf.Events</code>, <code>pf.OpenApi</code>), and so are the modules components are written
				with, so the same component compiles on both. The database (Postgres or SQLite), the key-value
				store, channels, live rooms and actors are the server platform's alone; jobs, cron and email are
				packages for it (<a href={Paths.ecosystem}>the ecosystem</a>).
			</p>

			<Heading id="repository">This repository</Heading>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>Where</th><th>What</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>rocx-cli/</code></td><td>rocx, in Roc: the transpiler and every command, <code>rocx dev</code> and <code>just dev</code>'s loop included, built into <code>bin/rocx</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>cli/</code></td><td>the command-line platform rocx is built on: files, commands, a watched directory tree, signals, and one wait for whichever comes first; a Rust host</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>web/</code></td><td>the browser's platform: its Roc modules (those both platforms share among them: components', and requests, endpoints, events, middleware), the Zig host, and the JavaScript runtime</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>server/</code></td><td>the server's platform: its Roc modules, the Rust host, and the build</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>packages/</code>, <code>examples/</code></td><td>the <a href={Paths.ecosystem}>ecosystem</a>'s packages, and complete <a href={Paths.examples}>example apps</a></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>site/</code></td><td>this site, in rocx: its pages in <code>routes/</code>; <code>just dev</code> serves them as you edit, and <code>site/build.mjs</code> (<code>just site</code>) builds the static site</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>editors/</code></td><td>the <code>.rocx</code> grammar, the VS Code extension and the Orca plugin</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>test/</code>, <code>web/test/</code>, <code>site/test/</code>, <code>server/test/</code></td><td>the transpiler and CLI, the browser's platform, the site in a real browser, the server's platform</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				<code>just setup</code> takes Zig 0.17.1 and Rust 1.85 or newer from your system (from Nix, say)
				when it has them, and downloads them into <code>.tools/</code> when it doesn't. Roc is a fork of
				a pinned nightly with the compiler fixes Roc Solid needs. <code>just setup</code> builds it from
				a clone of the fork when there is one, or downloads it prebuilt from the repository's releases
				(for macOS on Apple silicon and Linux on arm64). Scripts use that compiler, never the <code>roc</code> on
				your <code>PATH</code>. Node is needed only for the tests, the site's build and the VS Code
				extension.
			</p>
			<Code
				source={"just setup   # the Roc fork, the pinned nightly, Zig and Rust if need be, into .tools/; then bin/rocx\njust db      # a Postgres in Docker, for the server platform (prints its DATABASE_URL)\njust test    # everything: the transpiler, both platforms, the packages, the site in Chromium\njust check   # formatting, lint, and the site's, packages' and examples' type checks\njust dev     # this site, rendered per request, rebuilt and reloaded on save\njust site    # the static site, in site/dist"}
				numbers={Bool.False}
			/>
			<p>
				<code>bin/rocx</code> also has commands for the repository's own scripts: <code>rocx site dev</code>
				and <code>rocx site prepare</code> (this site), <code>rocx self-build</code> (builds
				<code>bin/rocx</code> again when its Roc changed) and <code>rocx internal</code> (the tests'
				calls into rocx's functions).
			</p>
		</NoHydration>
	</>

<style>
	/* The commands, a card each: how it is written, and what it does. */
	.commands {
		display: grid;
		gap: var(--space-3);
		margin: var(--space-4) 0;
	}

	.command {
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 0.9fr) minmax(0, 1.1fr);
		gap: var(--space-4);
		padding: var(--space-4) var(--space-5);
		border-radius: var(--radius);
		background: var(--bg-raised);
	}

	.command-usage {
		margin: 0;
		color: var(--accent);
		font: 600 var(--size-sm) / 1.6 var(--mono);
	}

	.command-usage span {
		display: block;
		color: var(--text-muted);
		font-weight: 400;
	}

	.command p:not(.command-usage) {
		margin: 0;
		color: var(--text-muted);
		font-size: var(--size-sm);
	}

	.command p:not(.command-usage) + p {
		margin-top: var(--space-2);
	}

	/* The compiler's report, as it prints it: its lines aren't wrapped. */
	.report :global(pre) {
		white-space: pre;
	}

	@media (max-width: 52rem) {
		.command {
			grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
		}
	}
</style>
